gether and this is  something that could happen before  you're even born we've seen so much  change you think about mitochondrial  disorders where a woman who carries a  mitochondrial condition even though she  herself might be well but her offspring  are a significant risk of potentially  really being affected by the condition  we have these new techniques which are  just starting where you transfer the  nucleus from her egg making sure you  don't take any mitochondria from her egg  into another woman's egg where there are  totally normal  mitochondria and remove and remove the  nucleus that's in that egg and then you  know she's able to have a child with  great reassurance that the child won't  have one of these devastating  mitochondrial diseases that level of  manipulation is happening so yes it did  it is on the horizon I think but with  that comes many ethical questions that  it could lead to social cleansing and a  race of superhumans available only to  those that could afford it I think  there'll always be a minority who want  to throw money of this loads of people  are dominated by very superficial ideas  of what people should look like and what  their capacities should be Tom  Shakespeare who was born with  achondroplasia says it's a dangerous  idea the number of people with  disabilities who've built the world we  live in is extraordinary you and I and  everybody else uses the internet and our  smartphones all the time who designed  that mostly people on the autistic  spectrum  Silicon Valley full of people on the  autistic spectrum this is where in a  heart of cultural London who drives the  culture that we love gay people you know  these are the diversity and differences  which make our lives better so we don't  need to start trying to make us all  homogenous white heterosexual  able-bodied six-foot tall boring dull  unimaginative people history shows  developments in technology have the  biggest impact in transforming care 70  years from now the possibilities are  almost limitless more effective more  personalized treatments whether for  cancer or chronic kidney disease  there are many incredible breakthroughs  within our grasp but it's private  companies that now hold the reins to  innovation the notion that you go to a  large place and a district General  Hospital or a or a GP clinic to receive  your care I think will change financing  healthcare will always be the greatest  challenge the vision for the future is  packed with possibility it's not like on  screen it will be in your glasses right  so so if we still use augmented reality  it will be what you see is you see  inside the patients but in solving new  problems and answering tough ethical  questions we'll need the same creativity  [Music]
 

